  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nWe will see quasi-zonal flow aloft across the forecast area \nthrough the day as surface high pressure encroaches from the \nsouthwest. This pattern will lead to another day of well above \nnormal highs mainly ranging from the lower 50s north to the \nlower 60s south and along the coast. Highs will be a bit cooler \non Saturday as the center of high pressure moves directly \noverhead, mainly in the upper 40s to the upper 50s. Minimal \ncloud cover and light winds will lead to some chilly lows, but \ncloser to normal for this time of year. Tonight's lows will \nmainly be in the low to mid 30s. Saturday night's lows will be \nsimilar but maybe just a touch warmer with less certainty in\nregards to cloud cover. Sunday will then see increasing clouds \nahead of the next system with highs mainly in the 50s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA surface low will start to deepen off the coast of the\nCarolinas during the day on Sunday before riding up the coast\nthrough Monday. While all global models and most of their\nrespective ensemble members show a general solution of a coastal\nstorm, much uncertainty remains with regards to specific\ndetails. All ensembles still show a wide variety of potential low\ntracks/positions which will have a significant impact on the\neventual outcome. However, confidence does continue to increase\nthat many areas will see a beneficial soaking rain during this \ntimeframe with the highest probabilities currently located over \nthe southern two thirds of New Hampshire and much of western \nMaine. Here, the latest NBM 24-hour probabilities for an inch or\nmore of rain range from 50 to near 80 percent. Increasing that \nthreshold to 2 inches and we see a large area of 30 to 50 \npercent probabilities along coastal/interior Maine and southern \nNew Hampshire.\n\nA cold front will cross the forecast area early in the day on\nMonday will will lead to winds shifting from out of the\nsoutheast to out of the northwest, becoming breezy by the \nafternoon hours. GFS soundings suggest the potential for some\nsites to see 25 to 35 knots at the top of the mixed layer. In\nthe unlikely event the the low tracks inland, we could see a\nbetter chance for strong southerly winds late Sunday through\nMonday morning. However, glancing at the many individual\nensemble member low tracks/positions, this appears to be a low \nprobability scenario as of now and the low should remain off\nshore.\n\nThe active pattern may well continue into mid week towards the\nThanksgiving holiday as long range guidance continues to \nsignal the chance for additional low pressure systems \norganizing in the eastern CONUS and moving up the coast. \nSolutions are even wider with this proposition, so confidence is\nlow, but this will overlap with the monthly high astronomical \ntide cycle. The high tides will make the coast susceptible to \ncoastal flooding if combined with swell and surge from a coastal\nlow. Those with interests along the coast should monitor coming\nforecasts should this threat become more likely.",
